WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Japan's rice price supports kept steep terraces farmed until aging left them abandoned to

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:13.860
bamboo and erosion. This is unintended consequences, episode 123. After the Second World War, Japanese

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.400
policymakers faced chronic food shortages, and a rural population still recovering from

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.700
wartime losses. They chose a system of price floors and acreage controls that guaranteed farmers

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.440
farmers a stable return for rice, regardless of the size or difficulty of their fields.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.900
The goal was straightforward, keep domestic production high enough to reduce reliance

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.800
on imports and preserve the social fabric of farming communities.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.860
At the time, most terraces were still worked by multi-generational households with abundant

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:51.560
family labor, so the policy appeared to reinforce both food security and landscape maintenance

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:52.200
at once.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:58.120
officials viewed the terraces themselves as an asset, delivering not only grain but

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.640
also flood buffering for villages downstream. The approach reflected the information available

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:09.840
then. Rice was the cultural staple, small holders were numerous, and import dependence carried

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.660
political risks. One can see why the arithmetic looked sound on paper. If a guaranteed price covered

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.960
the extra hours spent carrying seedlings up narrow paths and repairing stone walls by

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.540
hand, then even a half-hack-taire plot could contribute to national self-sufficiency without

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:33.400
requiring large machinery or consolidation. No one needed to assume the labor supply would

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:40.300
remain fixed. It simply matched the conditions visible in the late 1940s and 1950s when returning

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:46.420
soldiers and extended families still filled the mountain hamlets. The price support regime expanded

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:52.680
through the 1960s and 1970s under the Food Control Law and later adjustments by the Ministry

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:58.560
of Agriculture. Farmers received payments tied to production volume, which made cultivation

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.860
of marginal terraces economically rational even when yields per hour of labor were low.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.120
Early results showed rising self-sufficiency ratios and stable rural incomes,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.280
which proponents cited as proof the system worked. Some agricultural economists warned that the

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.380
supports would gradually detach production decisions from actual labor availability and

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.160
land quality.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:26.460
Yet, the political priority of protecting part-time farmers kept the framework in place.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:33.100
By the 1980s, the terraces remained green, and the downstream flood risk stayed low, masking

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.220
the slow demographic shift already underway in the mountains.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.680
The mechanism operated through annual negotiations that set a floor price well above import levels,

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:49.280
so a farmer could calculate that even a low yielding terrace would clear costs once the government purchase was assured.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:55.280
This calculation held as long as enough household members remained to perform the seasonal tasks.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:04.420
The policy did not track the age distribution of those workers or project when the next generation might leave for factory jobs in Osaka or Tokyo.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:10.620
As rural populations aged and younger residents move to cities, the supply of people willing

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.900
to plant, weed and repair terrace walls each season steadily declined.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.400
The price supports continue to make rice sales profitable on paper, but they could not

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.480
replace the physical labor required to keep water channels clear and in bankments intact.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.820
Abandon plots first grew weeds, then bamboo that spread upslope and altered drainage patterns.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.280
roads. Without regular maintenance, the terraces lost their ability to slow runoff, so heavy

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.380
rains sent more sediment and water into streams below. Villages that had counted on the

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.680
terraces for informal flood protection began to experience higher peak flows and occasional

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:54.000
damage to roads and homes. The second order effect was ecological. Once bamboo took hold,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:04:00.500
it proved difficult to remove, further discouraging any later return to cultivation. The third order

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.240
After effect reached national policy debates, where pressure to liberalize rice imports

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:10.320
collided with the visible decay of a landscape that the old supports had unintentionally preserved,

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.700
only while labor remained plentiful.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.420
Consider the chain step by step.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.860
A single terrace wall, if left unrepared for three seasons, allows soil to slump and water

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:22.980
to cut a new channel.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.740
That channel carries extra sediment that raises the bed of the stream below, so the next

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.020
heavy rain overtops banks that once contained it.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:37.520
Downstream residents then request concrete revetments whose cost is borne by prefectural

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.100
budgets rather than by the rice price mechanism.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:46.120
The original policy never priced those downstream costs because they appeared only after the

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:47.360
labor constraint tightened.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:53.580
By the 2000s, some prefectures began offering direct payments for tariffs upkeep separate

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.960
from the rice price mechanism, recognizing that commodity supports alone no longer sustained

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:04.540
the landscape functions. A few communities experimented with volunteer programs and tourism

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:10.600
linked farming to bring outside labor back to the slopes. The original price support system itself

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:16.160
was gradually scaled back under international trade agreements, yet the terraces that had already

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:22.260
gone follow did not automatically return to production. Today many remain in varying states of

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.700
collect, while downstream flood management budgets have grown to compensate for the lost

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.820
buffering capacity.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.260
Even the newer direct payment schemes face the same arithmetic problem.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.160
They compensate for maintenance but still compete with urban wages for the time of younger

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.900
residents, so uptake remains patchy on the steepest slopes.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.420
Incentive structures that reward an output without securing the inputs needed to produce it

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.560
will eventually confront a labor or resource constraint.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:55.100
Right, complex landscapes deliver multiple services at once, and a single commodity price

00:05:55.100 --> 00:06:00.500
signal can mask the gradual loss of those other services until the costs appear elsewhere.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.580
When designing supports for any activity that also maintains shared infrastructure, it helps

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:11.560
to track the underlying labor and demographic trends rather than assuming they will remain

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:12.180
constant.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.460
How might current policies that stabilize prices or incomes in other sectors be interacting

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.220
with similar, slow-moving demographic or ecological changes today.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.380
That's unintended consequences for today.

